## Proof

Let $n$ be an even positive integer. The square in row $k$, column $j$ (for $k,j \in \{1,2,\dots,n\}$) contains the number
$$a_{k,j} = (k-1)n + j.$$

Let us assign to each square a sign
$$\varepsilon_{k,j} = \begin{cases} +1 & \text{if square $(k,j)$ is red}, \\ -1 & \text{if square $(k,j)$ is black}. \end{cases}$$

The difference between the red sum and the black sum is
$$D = \sum_{\substack{k,j \\ \text{red}}} a_{k,j} \;-\; \sum_{\substack{k,j \\ \text{black}}} a_{k,j} = \sum_{k=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n} \varepsilon_{k,j}\, a_{k,j}.$$

Substituting $a_{k,j} = (k-1)n + j$ and splitting:
$$D = \sum_{k=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n} \varepsilon_{k,j}\bigl((k-1)n + j\bigr) = n\sum_{k=1}^{n}(k-1)\!\sum_{j=1}^{n}\varepsilon_{k,j} \;+\; \sum_{j=1}^{n} j\!\sum_{k=1}^{n}\varepsilon_{k,j}.$$

**Row condition.** In every row $k$, exactly $n/2$ squares are red and $n/2$ are black, so
$$\sum_{j=1}^{n} \varepsilon_{k,j} = \tfrac{n}{2}(+1) + \tfrac{n}{2}(-1) = 0 \qquad \text{for each } k.$$

**Column condition.** In every column $j$, exactly $n/2$ squares are red and $n/2$ are black, so
$$\sum_{k=1}^{n} \varepsilon_{k,j} = \tfrac{n}{2}(+1) + \tfrac{n}{2}(-1) = 0 \qquad \text{for each } j.$$

The row condition annihilates the first term of $D$, and the column condition annihilates the second term. Hence
$$D = n\sum_{k=1}^{n}(k-1)\cdot 0 \;+\; \sum_{j=1}^{n} j\cdot 0 = 0.$$

Therefore the sum of the numbers on the red squares equals the sum of the numbers on the black squares, for every coloring satisfying the given row and column balance conditions. $\blacksquare$

